Architecture
study-workstation.html          Single-file app (HTML + CSS + JS, ~907 lines)
│
├── Tab: Board                  Spatial index grid (A–Z × demand-driven rows)
├── Tab: Library                Full card list with search/filter
├── Tab: Retrieve               RAG retrieve stage — keyword scoring
├── Tab: Assistant              AI-assisted card creation and study advice
├── Tab: Study                  Active recall flashcard mode
└── Tab: Session                Pomodoro-aligned session timer and log
│
└── localStorage["gridindex-v1"]  Sparse JSON record store (no server required)
Data layer: Records are stored sparsely — only cells that hold at least one card exist in the data store. The board renders on demand from the data, not from a pre-allocated fixed grid.

Board — Current Behaviour (v12)
The board currently renders a fixed 7-column × 6-row grid (columns A–G, rows 1–6). Coordinates are validated against the pattern ^[A-G][1-6]$. The hint text reads: "A 7×6 index (A1–G6). Each card has a fixed address. Tap a cell to see or add cards."

Team Meeting — 2026-09-13
Attendees: Shannon Brian Kelly (Architect), Claude (Coding Assistant)
Topic: Board elastic extension; accessibility research review; note card pedagogy decision
Decisions made
Expand columns from A–G to A–Z (26 columns).
The full Latin alphabet provides a natural, memorable, and dyslexia-compatible addressing system. No numbering system required for columns.
Expand rows from fixed 1–6 to demand-driven (potentially 1–100+).
Rows render only when occupied content exists, plus a configurable buffer of empty rows below the lowest occupied row. No pre-allocation. Cells collapse back visually when all their cards are deleted.
Note card creation: student-controlled by default; assistant-on-request.
Evidence basis: the Generation Effect (Slamecka & Graf, 1978) shows student-generated content is retained significantly better than passively received content. UDL graduated scaffolding principle: maximum student agency at the baseline; scaffolding available on explicit request.
Implementation: the student manually enters a card coordinate. The assistant may suggest or place a card if the student explicitly asks ("put this in the right cell for me"). When the assistant places a card, it must display its reasoning (e.g., "I put this at C3 because it matches your other retrieval notes").
All design changes require documentation before code changes. The documentation gate remains in effect (see below).
README.md becomes the first source of truth for the elastic board extension. After README is committed to GitHub, pseudocode becomes the second source of truth, then code is updated.

New Code Required — Next Implementation Sprint
The following changes are specified and approved by the architect. No code is to be modified until this README is committed and pseudocode is written.
Change 1 — Expand address space to A–Z × demand-driven rows
Current code (lines ~19 of script):
const COLS=["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"], ROWS=[1,2,3,4,5,6];
Replacement:
const COLS = Array.from({length: 26}, (_, i) => String.fromCharCode(65 + i));
// ROWS is no longer a constant — derived at render time from occupied data

Change 2 — Update coordinate validator in editCard
Current:
if(!/^[A-G][1-6]$/.test(coordV)){toast("The cell must be A1 through G6.");return;}
Replacement:
if(!/^[A-Z]\d{1,3}$/.test(coordV) || parseInt(coordV.slice(1)) < 1){
  toast("Cell must be a letter A–Z followed by a row number (e.g. A1, Z42).");
  return;
}

Change 3 — Update coordinate default in newCard
Current:
coord: /^[A-G][1-6]$/.test(o.coord) ? o.coord : "D3",
Replacement:
coord: /^[A-Z]\d{1,3}$/.test(o.coord) && parseInt(o.coord.slice(1)) >= 1
       ? o.coord : "D3",

Change 4 — Demand-driven board renderer
The board render function must be rewritten to:
Collect all occupied row numbers from STATE.records
Compute a render range: min occupied row down to max occupied row, plus a buffer of 3 empty rows below
Render only that row range (no fixed loop to 6)
Always include at least rows 1–6 when the data store is empty (blank-slate default)
Pseudocode outline (to be fully specified in STUDY_WORKSTATION_RECORD_AND_PSEUDOCODE.md):
function getRowsToRender(records, buffer = 3):
    occupied = unique row numbers from records (parsed integer)
    if occupied is empty: return [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
    maxRow = max(occupied)
    return range(1, maxRow + buffer)

function renderBoard():
    rows = getRowsToRender(STATE.records)
    cols = COLS  // A–Z
    for each row in rows:
        for each col in cols:
            coord = col + row
            cards = listAt(coord)
            render cell(coord, cards)

Change 5 — Update Board hint text (HTML)
Current:
<p class="hint">A 7×6 index (A1–G6). Each card has a fixed address. Tap a cell to see or add cards.</p>
Replacement:
<p class="hint">A–Z columns × rows that grow as you add cards. Each card has a fixed address. Tap a cell to see or add cards — the board expands when you do.</p>

Change 6 — Assistant card placement reasoning display
When the assistant places a card at a coordinate (assistant-on-request mode), the UI must display a one-line explanation beneath the card in the Board cell, e.g.:
"Placed at C3 — matches your other retrieval-practice notes in column C."
This text should be visually distinct (italic, reduced opacity) and dismissible.

